"Places significant delivery events and decision gates in time without expanding the full activity schedule.\n\nUse when: Communicate major commitments to sponsors or partner teams. Coordinate acceptance events and cross-project handoffs.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need task-level duration, capacity or a reliable calculation of schedule criticality.\n\nVisual grammar: Milestone markers denote observable zero-duration events. A time axis shows dated commitments or explicitly labeled forecast windows. Rows group initiatives or outcomes without implying equal task effort.\n\nBuild: Select a small set of externally meaningful achievements or gates. Define completion evidence and owners for each milestone. Position dates and distinguish committed, forecast and uncertain timing.\n\nCheck: Every milestone describes a verifiable event. Dates use a consistent calendar and stated status. The roadmap preserves material dependencies without becoming a detailed schedule.\n\nPitfalls: Calling an ongoing activity a milestone. Presenting speculative dates with the same certainty as approved commitments.\n\nExample: A visitor center roadmap marks permit approval, handover, safety acceptance and public opening, with forecast windows for unresolved inspections.\n\nAlternatives: Gantt chart, Now-next-later roadmap."
